@section('content')
<div class="not-prose">
<p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">Parked on the wrong side of the street when the plows came through? Woke up to find your car buried, or slid into a ditch on the way home? Here is how snow season parking works in Spokane, what can get a car ticketed or towed, and what to do when your car is stuck.</p>
<div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-red-500/60 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><em>Note: Spokane Towing is a towing referral line. We aren't the city, the police or an impound lot. This is a plain-language guide based on City of Spokane, City of Spokane Valley, Spokane County, WSDOT, Washington State Patrol and National Weather Service sources, linked below. Rules can change, so check the source if anything is unclear.</em></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Quick answer: snow season parking and what to do</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-slate-800 rounded-lg"><thead class="bg-slate-900/70 text-white"><tr><th class="p-3">Situation</th><th class="p-3">What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-gray-300">
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">City of Spokane residential street, Nov. 15 – March 15</td><td class="p-3">Park on the <strong class="text-white">odd-numbered address side</strong> of the street (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Downtown Spokane on a snowy day</td><td class="p-3">No on-street parking from <strong class="text-white">midnight to 6 a.m.</strong> when the City announces it. Cars left there can be towed (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Any City of Spokane street after a plowing notice</td><td class="p-3">Don't park there. Parking is prohibited once the City gives public notice it will remove snow (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/smc/?Section=16A.61.564" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SMC 16A.61.564</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">RV, boat or trailer on a Spokane street</td><td class="p-3">Move it to winter storage. These can't stay on the street more than 24 hours (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/releases/2026/11/24/city-street-department-outlines-winter-operations/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 2026</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Spokane Valley</td><td class="p-3">Keep vehicles off the street so plows can get through. Report snow concerns through <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://spokanevalleywa.gov/443/SVexpress---Report-a-Concern" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SVexpress</a> or (509) 720-5000</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Your car is gone after plowing</td><td class="p-3">Call Crime Check, (509) 456-2233. See <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towed-car-spokane">how to find a towed car</a></td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Stuck in snow or in a ditch, no one hurt, out of traffic</td><td class="p-3">Stay in the car, hazards on, then call for a <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/winch-out-recovery">winch-out</a>: (509) 797-7999</td></tr>
<tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Anyone hurt, car in a traffic lane, or you're in danger</td><td class="p-3">Call 911</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">How the City of Spokane plows snow</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">The City's snow season runs from <strong class="text-white">Nov. 15 to March 15</strong>, and this winter's operations continue through March 15, 2027 (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/releases/2026/11/24/city-street-department-outlines-winter-operations/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 24, 2026</a>). The City's current snow page doesn't describe numbered snow emergency stages. Instead, it describes two levels of plowing (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane: Street Snow Removal</a>):</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
<div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Maintenance plow (a couple of inches)</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Crews plow residential streets as well as arterials, mostly during regular weekday day shifts. Arterials go first, then residential hills.</p></div>
<div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Full-City Plow (four inches or more)</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Crews switch to 24/7 operations. The City expects a Full-City Plow to take about three days, and a plow progress map goes live during these events.</p></div>
</div>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Priorities are arterials, hills, hospitals and school routes. Hill neighborhoods are plowed before flat ones, and the first residential area plowed rotates each time. Cul-de-sacs are plowed separately with special equipment. If conditions get so bad that crews can't keep arterials clear, the City switches to its signed <strong class="text-white">Snow Corridor Plan</strong> and concentrates on those routes until conditions improve (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>).</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Spokane snow parking rules (City of Spokane)</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Residential streets: park on the odd side.</strong> For the whole snow season, residents are asked to park on the odd-numbered address side of the street. The City says this lets plows make enough room for emergency response vehicles and keeps more snow off sidewalks (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/utilities/inserts/2026/11/november-2026-utility-billing-insert.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 2026 utility insert</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Downtown: off the street from midnight to 6 a.m. on snowy days.</strong> When the City announces it, on-street parking downtown is prohibited from midnight to 6 a.m. so parking bays can be plowed out, and the City says vehicles parked there can be towed during noticed events. The area is generally Maple to Division and I-90 to the Spokane River, not including the residential-only part of Peaceful Valley. Signs are posted downtown, and the City notes some snow-day parking under the freeway plus off-street surface lots (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">After a plowing notice, any street.</strong> City code says no one may park, or leave a vehicle parked, on a street after the City has given public notice (press, radio, TV or notice to the neighborhood) that it intends to remove snow there (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/smc/?Section=16A.61.564" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SMC 16A.61.564</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">RVs, boats and trailers.</strong> Move them, and basketball hoops, off the street to winter storage before snow season. RVs, boats and trailers can't be parked on the street for more than 24 hours in a row (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/releases/2026/11/24/city-street-department-outlines-winter-operations/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 24, 2026</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">How you'll hear about it.</strong> The City shares parking restrictions with local media, on its social media, and through MySpokane 311 (call 311, or (509) 755-2489 from outside city limits). Live phone help is 7 a.m. to 6 p.m. Monday to Friday, with some weekend hours during Full-City Plow events (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/streets/maintenance/snow-removal/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>).</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Spokane Valley and Spokane County</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Spokane Valley</strong> runs its own plowing. Main arterials, critical intersections and hillside roads are plowed whenever snow builds up, often overnight when traffic is light. Residential streets on the valley floor are plowed when snow significantly reduces traffic flow, and a full residential plow takes about 48 hours. Trent Avenue (SR 290) and Pines Road (SR 27) are plowed by WSDOT (509-324-6000), and the City doesn't plow private roads (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.spokanevalleywa.gov/322/Snow-Removal" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane Valley: Snow Removal</a>). Its snow page doesn't list an odd-side parking rule; the City asks residents to make room for plows by keeping vehicles and other obstructions off the street. Pushing snow from your driveway or sidewalk into the street is a misdemeanor there.</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Unincorporated Spokane County</strong> roads are plowed by County Public Works in priority order: major arterials, then secondary arterials, then residential roads (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.spokanecounty.gov/882/Winter-Roads-Maintenance" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane County: Winter Roads Maintenance</a>). The County asks residents to get vehicles, watercraft and basketball hoops off the street so plows can work (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.spokanecounty.gov/civicalerts.aspx" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane County</a>), and county code says that outside cities and towns no one may park or leave a vehicle standing on the roadway, except a disabled vehicle that has to be moved promptly (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://library.municode.com/wa/spokane_county/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=TIT46MOVE_CH46.61RURO_46.61.540STSTPAOUBUREDI" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane County Code 46.61.540</a>). Questions: Spokane County Public Works, (509) 477-3600.</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">State highways and I-90.</strong> The Washington State Patrol says a vehicle left on most state roads can be impounded after 24 hours, or right away if it is a traffic hazard or blocks traffic, <strong class="text-white">including snowplows</strong>. A car left on the left shoulder (median) can also be impounded right away (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsp.wa.gov/i-want-to/frequently-asked-questions/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSP FAQ</a>).</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Car missing after the plows came through?</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Call <strong class="text-white">Crime Check at (509) 456-2233</strong>, the Spokane-area non-emergency police line. They can tell you whether the car was reported towed or impounded and which tow company has it, or take a stolen-vehicle report. If it was on a state highway, the State Patrol says to contact the local WSP office (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsp.wa.gov/i-want-to/frequently-asked-questions/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSP FAQ</a>). For the full steps (getting it back, fees, and how to contest a tow), see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towed-car-spokane">car towed in Spokane? how to find it</a>.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Car stuck in snow or slid into a ditch: what to do</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Call 911 if</strong> anyone is hurt, your car is in a traffic lane or another dangerous spot, or there was a crash with another vehicle. If you're in a collision, the State Patrol says the safest place to be is in your vehicle with your seat belt on (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsp.wa.gov/traveler/docs/equipmt/winter_driving_tips.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSP</a>). For crash situations, see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/accident-towing-spokane">accident towing in Spokane</a>.</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">If no one is hurt and you're out of traffic:</strong></p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-4">
<li>Stay with the vehicle. The National Weather Service says leaving it in wind-driven snow and cold can leave you disoriented quickly (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.weather.gov/safety/winter-during" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">NWS</a>).</li>
<li>Turn on your hazard lights. If you run the engine for heat, the NWS suggests about 10 minutes each hour with a window cracked open, and clearing snow from the exhaust pipe first. A blocked tailpipe can fill the car with carbon monoxide, which you can't see or smell (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.cdc.gov/natural-disasters/psa-toolkit/clear-snow-from-tailpipes.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">CDC</a>).</li>
<li>Don't wear yourself out pushing the car or digging in deep snow. The NWS warns the strain and cold can bring on a heart attack, and sweating can lead to hypothermia.</li>
<li>Call for help with your exact location: road name, nearest cross street or mile marker, and direction of travel.</li>
</ul>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Getting it out.</strong> A car in a ditch, snowbank or snowy lot often just needs a <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/winch-out-recovery">winch-out</a>, not a full tow. If the cold left you with a dead battery, ask for a <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/battery-jump-start">jump start</a>. For flats, lockouts or running out of gas, see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/roadside-assistance">roadside assistance</a>. If the car is damaged or won't drive after it's pulled out, a partner can tow it to your home or mechanic.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Winter driving around Spokane</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Check before you go.</strong> The <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://srtmc.org/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane Regional Transportation Management Center</a> posts local traffic cameras and winter road reports for I-90, US 2, US 395 and US 195 around Spokane. WSDOT's <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsdot.com/travel/real-time/map/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">real-time travel map</a> and <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsdot.com/travel/real-time/mountainpasses" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">mountain pass reports</a> cover the rest of the state, and the WSDOT app has the same information.</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Know which roads get plowed first.</strong> WSDOT's top priority is keeping interstates like I-90 open, aiming for at least one lane each way from the start of a storm (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsdot.wa.gov/travel/operations-services/snow-and-ice-plan" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSDOT snow and ice plan</a>). In the city, arterials and hills come before flat residential streets. If you live on a hill or a side street, expect it to stay snowy longer during a big storm.</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Give plows room.</strong> Don't pass a plow; operators can't see well and may be throwing snow in either direction (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsdot.wa.gov/travel/operations-services/snow-and-ice-plan" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSDOT</a>). WSDOT's winter guide says to stay back about 15 car lengths, skip cruise control, and slow down near intersections, off-ramps, bridges and shady spots (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.wsdot.wa.gov/publications/fulltext/winter/winter-driving-guide.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSDOT Winter Driving Guide</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Tires and chains.</strong> Studded tires are legal in Washington only from Nov. 1 through March 31, and they don't count as chains when chains are required. Traction tires need at least 1/8 inch of tread and the mountain-and-snowflake symbol (or M+S / All Season). Skipping chains when they're required can mean a $500 fine (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://wsdot.wa.gov/about/news/2026/wsdot-storm-ready-winter-are-you" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSDOT, Oct. 2026</a>).</p>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Pack a winter kit.</strong> WSDOT suggests a full tank of gas, a flashlight, jumper cables, flares, an ice scraper and snow brush, a first aid kit, a phone charger, water and snacks, warm clothes and tire chains, and a winter check-up of your tires, battery, wipers, lights and heater (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.wsdot.wa.gov/publications/fulltext/winter/winter-driving-guide.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WSDOT</a>). Spokane County adds traction sand or cat litter, a shovel and blankets (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.spokanecounty.gov/civicalerts.aspx" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane County</a>). Clear snow off your windows and roof before you drive.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Need your car pulled out or towed?</h2>
<p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Call <strong class="text-white">(509) 797-7999</strong> and we'll connect you with a local partner tow company for a winch-out, jump start or tow anywhere in Spokane, Spokane Valley and nearby. Tell us whether you're in deep snow, on ice or in a ditch so the right truck is sent. Arrival times vary, especially during storms, and you get an exact ETA on the call. Online estimates use a <strong class="text-white">$140 hook fee plus $6 per mile, with a $200 minimum</strong>; see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towing-cost-spokane">Spokane towing cost</a> for examples, or the full list of <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/spokane-towing-services">Spokane towing services</a>.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="divide-y divide-slate-800 border-y border-slate-800">
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Which side of the street do I park on in Spokane during snow season?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Inside the City of Spokane, residents are asked to park on the odd-numbered address side of residential streets for the whole snow season, Nov. 15 to March 15. That leaves room for plows and emergency vehicles.</p></div>
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Can my car be towed for parking on the street when it snows in Spokane?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">It can. City code (SMC 16A.61.564) says no one may park on a street after the City has given public notice that it intends to remove snow there. Downtown, on-street parking is prohibited from midnight to 6 a.m. on snowy days when announced, and the City says vehicles parked there can be towed during noticed events.</p></div>
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How do I find out if my car was towed during snow plowing?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Call Crime Check, the Spokane-area non-emergency police line, at (509) 456-2233. They can tell you whether the car was reported towed or impounded and which tow company has it. If it was left on a state highway, contact the Washington State Patrol.</p></div>
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Does Spokane Valley have an odd-side parking rule?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">The City of Spokane Valley's snow removal page doesn't list an odd- or even-side parking rule. It asks residents to make room for plows by keeping vehicles off the street, plows main arterials, hillside roads and critical intersections first, and says a full residential plow takes about 48 hours.</p></div>
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">What should I do if my car slides into a ditch in the snow?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">If anyone is hurt, the car is in a traffic lane, or you are in danger, call 911. Otherwise stay in the vehicle with your seat belt on, turn on your hazards, and keep the exhaust pipe clear of snow if you run the engine. Then call for a winch-out. On a state highway, the State Patrol can impound a vehicle right away if it blocks traffic or snowplows.</p></div>
<div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How long does it take Spokane to plow residential streets?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">When it snows a couple of inches, City crews plow residential streets mostly on weekday day shifts. For a snowfall of four inches or more, crews go to 24/7 operations for a Full-City Plow, which the City says takes about three days. Arterials, hills, hospitals and school routes come first.</p></div>
</div>
